Whats working : 

The work described in the MuJoCo force-control README establishes a simulated
Franka Panda environment with joint position and velocity sensors and a
joint-space PD controller. It provides actuator force feedback and checks for
commanded and measured joint torques against configured actuator limits. An
extended Kalman filter and a sliding-mode observer estimate joint-space
disturbance torques from the simulated dynamics. A simulated six-axis load cell
and Cartesian force controller provide end-effector wrench sensing and
feedback. Hybrid position/force, cascaded force, impedance, and admittance
controllers, together with regression tests, complete the current simulation
foundation.

This foundation can be extended with a peg, hole, contact model, and
independently measured insertion success criteria to create a repeatable test
environment. The existing sensors and controllers can support contact
experiments and classical baselines, while their limits and simulated
load-cell behavior must be validated before real-world use. A fixed pi0.5
vision-language-action model is planned to supply nominal actions, with a
smaller residual policy providing bounded corrections. Four residual-policy
variants will separately enable or disable force observations and force-aware
reward shaping, while holding the backbone, action interface, safety checks,
and evaluation conditions consistent. An extended goal could be that after simulation validation and
independent hardware safety checks, randomized real-robot trials can compare
success rates, force-related outcomes, and the interaction between the two
factors.

# Research Outline: Force-Aware Residual Policy for Peg-in-Hole Insertion

## 1. Research Question

Does force information in the residual policy's observations and force-aware
reward shaping independently improve real-world peg-in-hole success, and do
they interact, when the same pretrained pi0.5 vision-language-action (VLA)
model provides the nominal action?

## 2. Motivation and Hypothesis

Peg insertion requires visual alignment, compliant contact, and controlled
motion after visual feedback becomes insufficient. A fixed pi0.5 policy
provides a consistent nominal action; a smaller learned residual policy
corrects that action. The study isolates whether (a) force observation,
(b) force-aware reward shaping, or (c) their combination improves insertion
performance.

Primary hypotheses:

1. Force observations improve robustness during contact and insertion.
2. A task-progress reward with a force penalty improves insertion without
   encouraging the policy to avoid necessary contact.
3. Using both produces a measurable interaction beyond either factor alone.

## 3. Task and Success Criteria

Use an 8 mm diameter peg with a specified 0.08 mm radial peg-hole clearance.
The target insertion depth is 25 mm. A trial meets the depth criterion when
its measured insertion depth is between 20 mm and 25 mm inclusive, and the
lateral criterion when the measured center offset is no more than 0.08 mm.
Define success independently of the learned reward and require both criteria,
a specified seated dwell time, and no predefined hard force-limit violation
or damage. Record each condition separately.

With a nominal 8.16 mm hole diameter, 0.08 mm is the ideal center-offset
clearance for a rigid, aligned 8 mm peg; tilt and manufacturing tolerances can
reduce the practical margin. Before implementing the evaluator, specify the
lateral measurement reference and frame, and verify that a success
measurement cannot count a peg beside the hole as inserted. Confirm the
depicted dimensions and depth direction, and specify how insertion and seated
dwell time will be independently measured.

## 4. Experimental Factors and Ablation

Keep the pi0.5 checkpoint, robot/action interface, demonstration data, training
budget, controller limits, and evaluation distribution fixed. Train/evaluate
the four residual-policy variants:

| Variant | Force in residual observation | Force-aware reward |
|---|---|---|
| Baseline | Off | Off |
| Observation only | On | Off |
| Reward only | Off | On |
| Combined | On | On |

Use multiple training seeds. Randomize peg/hole pose, initial alignment,
lighting, and other relevant trial conditions consistently across variants.
Use paired trials where practical.

## 5. Policy, Observation, and Action Interface

1. Verify the selected pi0.5 checkpoint, supported observation/action schema,
   license, inference hardware, and fine-tuning path from the official OpenPI
   documentation before committing to the integration.
2. Define a timestamped observation contract: camera image(s), joint position
   and velocity, gripper state, end-effector pose, and (for force-on variants)
   calibrated six-axis wrist force/torque with frame, units, filtering, and
   saturation status.
3. Define the residual action in a bounded, documented space (for example,
   Cartesian pose delta or twist), its frame, scale, update rate, and horizon.
   Combine it with pi0.5's nominal action through a deterministic adapter.
4. Preserve identical non-force observations and actions across ablations.
   When force is off, do not leak force readings through other features.
5. Normalize using training data only and save transforms/statistics with
   checkpoint and experiment metadata.

The existing MuJoCo implementation provides joint sensors, actuator force
feedback, torque observers, a six-axis end-effector load cell, joint PD control,
Cartesian wrench control, hybrid position/force control, cascaded force
control, impedance/admittance controllers, and regression tests. Reuse these
as simulation infrastructure, not as evidence that pi0.5 is already integrated
or that the simulated load cell equals a physical sensor.

## 6. Reward and Safety

Define reward terms before training. Use task progress (alignment and insertion
depth) and terminal success; in force-aware variants add a calibrated contact
quality/force term and penalties for excessive force, impacts, unstable
contact, and wasted motion. Do not reward simply minimizing force, because
insertion requires contact. Keep terminal success and safety evaluation
independent of the shaped reward.

Enforce hard force, torque, workspace, speed, and emergency-stop limits in the
low-level controller for every variant. Learned policy outputs must be
validated, bounded, and stopped on stale observations, sensor faults, or
communication loss. Do not rely on reward shaping for safety.

## 7. Staged Implementation Plan

### Stage A: Validate task definitions and protocol

Status: approved for MuJoCo simulation experiments only. The user confirmed
that no real robot is being used. This approval does not authorize real-robot
contact, establish physical safety limits, or validate the simulator as a
hardware-safety system. Any future hardware study requires a separate protocol
and safety review.

#### A1. Task geometry and evaluator

- Use the MuJoCo task model's 8 mm diameter, 60 mm long peg and nominal
  8.16 mm diameter, 30 mm deep hole (0.08 mm nominal radial clearance).
  The hole is represented by 64 faceted walls, so report this as a model
  approximation, not physical geometry or tolerance verification.
- A geometric success candidate must cross the hole entrance, reach 20-25 mm
  insertion depth inclusive, have no more than 0.08 mm axis offset at the
  entrance, and no more than 0.2 degrees angular error. Require 0.1 seconds of
  continuous valid geometry. The evaluator also checks conservative peg
  clearance along the inserted portion; position alone cannot count a peg
  beside the hole as inserted.
- Use a hole-fixed frame with origin at the entrance and positive depth directed
  into the hole. Compute lateral error from the peg-axis intersection with the
  entrance plane. Use MuJoCo ground truth for simulator scoring and record
  depth, lateral error, angular error, dwell, contact, and termination reason
  separately. The 1e-12 m allowance is numerical roundoff only.
- Simulator geometry is exact relative to the modeled shapes, not evidence that
  the model matches physical parts. Physical dimensions, tolerances, and
  measurement uncertainty remain out of scope until hardware is selected.

#### A2. Hardware and calibration record

The approved setup is the existing MuJoCo Franka Panda task scene: a simulated
finger grasp, kinematic fixture and collidable table, two fixed workspace
cameras, a provisional hand camera, and a simulated six-axis hand wrench
sensor. Trials start with the peg already grasped; pickup is not part of this
protocol. Use the scene's named frames and simulated timestamps. Camera
calibration, sensor noise/latency, and wrench interpretation are model
properties, not physical calibration claims. The hand sensor reports a
transmitted wrench, while the evaluator's peg/environment contact force is a
separate ground-truth signal.

There is no physical robot, gripper, fixture, camera, or force sensor in this
study. Physical frame calibration, sensor characterization, uncertainty
budgets, and safety limits are not applicable and remain prerequisites for any
future hardware protocol.

#### A3. Trial procedure and randomization

1. Run the robot-driven `position`, `hybrid`, or `impedance` baseline with the
   fixture fixed. Set the peg in the documented grasped start state; settle
   for 0.5 seconds and require contact with both fingers before starting the
   scored episode.
2. Use non-negative integer seeds. For each seed, all compared controllers
   receive the same independently sampled fixture X/Y offsets in [-0.05,
   0.05] mm and tilt components about world X/Y in [-0.03, 0.03] degrees.
   The table stays horizontal; lighting is fixed. Record seed and realized
   pose. These ranges define a narrow simulation benchmark, not a robustness
   or physical-reachability claim.
3. Use the same task configuration and randomization for each controller;
   record controller, code/configuration revision, MuJoCo version, and
   simulator seed. Use disjoint seeds for tuning and held-out evaluation.
4. Start the 12-second episode after the initial grasp check. The nominal
   downward Cartesian command is 0.005 m/s. End on evaluator success, force
   stop, workspace violation, dropped peg, invalid simulation state, or
   timeout. Preserve every started trial. A failed initial grasp is an invalid
   start, must be recorded, and must not be silently counted as a task failure
   or replaced without reporting it.
5. Record outcome, termination reason, final pose and geometry, contact-force
   summaries, and any invalid-start/abort status. Do not remove timeouts,
   force stops, drops, or other unfavorable outcomes from results.

#### A4. Safety and stopping rules

- Use the simulation evaluator's 5 N peg/environment contact-force threshold,
  1 N analysis reference, 12-second timeout, and workspace bounds of +/-0.3 m
  in X/Y and -0.05 to +0.3 m in Z relative to the hole entrance. The 1 N
  reference is an analysis threshold, not a safety threshold. Contact force
  is the sum of magnitudes at peg/hole and peg/table contacts; finger contacts
  are excluded. Log the net world-frame force separately when available.
- The 5 N stop is checked after a physics step and can overshoot substantially;
  it is a simulation termination condition, not a force cap or protective
  safety mechanism. Keep reported peaks, including overshoots, and label any
  force-stop trial as a failure. The current benchmark uses actuator/model
  saturation but does not establish independent joint-torque or actual-speed
  safety guarantees. The 0.005 m/s value is the commanded approach rate, not
  an enforced speed limit.
- Stop and report invalid simulation state, force-stop, workspace violation,
  dropped peg, or timeout. This simulation has no operator emergency-stop,
  communication-loss, or hardware fault-recovery test. Do not run physical
  contact under this protocol.
- For force analysis, use the evaluator's ground-truth peg/environment contact
  force at the physics step, sampled at the simulation timestep. Count samples
  strictly above Fref=1 N and merge contiguous samples into intervals; report
  interval edges to one physics-step resolution. Report the maximum interval
  and peak force separately. Keep the local hand-wrench signal distinct from
  this contact-force metric. If reporting failure categories, retain geometric
  and force labels independently; overlapping force labels use the documented
  excessive-pressing > unstable-contact > stalled precedence.

#### A5. Trial data contract

- Store each run in a new, non-overwriting directory under
  `mujoco_force_control/results/`; keep the manifest/summary and any full-rate
  trace together. Record schema version, run ID, controller/variant,
  checkpoint if applicable, training and trial seeds, software/model versions,
  geometry, randomized initial conditions, and simulation-time base.
- At each recorded sample, retain simulation time, camera images or stable
  references, joint position/velocity, gripper state, peg/hole pose, nominal
  and residual actions when applicable, executed control, local wrench and
  contact force as distinct fields, contact/task phase, and termination or
  sensor-status events. Record sample rates; do not align different-rate
  streams by array index.
- For each trial, retain success and each geometric check, final depth/lateral/
  angular errors, seating dwell, duration, peak force, intervals above Fref,
  retries if applicable, independent failure labels, termination reason, and
  invalid-start/exclusion reason. Preserve raw and processed signals and all
  failed trials.
- Force may be logged for analysis in every policy variant, but must not be
  exposed to residual observations when the force-observation factor is off.
  The approved study is simulation-only and contains no human or personal
  data; retain outputs with their provenance for reproducibility.

Stage A exit: the task definition, simulator setup, trial/randomization
procedure, simulation stopping/analysis rules, and data contract above are
approved for simulation-only experiments. Stage A is complete for that scope.
Training readiness still depends on Stage C's action conversion and dataset
validation. Real-robot hardware specification, measurement/calibration
validation, numeric physical limits, and safety approval remain explicitly
unresolved; no real-robot contact is authorized.

### Stage B: Establish the simulator benchmark

Status: complete as a reproducible MuJoCo benchmark prototype, not as a
hardware-ready system or statistically conclusive comparison. The Panda task
scene, free peg, simulated grasp, table, faceted hole, cameras, and seeded
fixture offsets are in place. `PegEpisode` checks entrance crossing, insertion
geometry and continuous seating dwell, plus force, drop, workspace, invalid
state, and timeout termination. Intentional release is explicit. Contact
samples, peak force, and intervals above the analysis reference are recorded
through termination.

The evaluator defaults are Fref = 1 N, a 5 N force-stop threshold, and a
20-second timeout; the paired robot-driven benchmark explicitly uses a
12-second timeout. Scripted kinematic fixture probes at 0.01 and 0.02 m/s
measured aligned peaks of 0.287 and 0 N, jam peaks of 10.645 and 15.583 N,
and pressing peaks of 5.720 and 6.268 N, respectively. Raw traces and exact
commands are in `mujoco_force_control/results/force_probes/` and the project
README. These probes are diagnostic, not statistical validation or safety
limits. Contact, friction, and grasp parameters remain provisional; reset
starts with the peg between the fingers rather than executing a pickup.

The Stage B trial starts with an already-grasped peg, checks initial contact
with both fingers, and keeps the fixture fixed while the robot inserts.
Robot-driven position, hybrid position/force, and Cartesian impedance
baselines now run on paired seeded fixture offsets and small fixture tilts.
The benchmark supplies reproducible Gaussian wrist-wrench noise and fixed
latency; camera and encoder observations remain ideal. It records termination
reasons, force summaries and heuristic failure labels, with optional per-step
traces. Labels deliberately leave ambiguous final geometry unclassified.
Seeded reset, geometry/tolerance boundaries, collision clearance, force
accounting, failure termination, noise/latency behavior, paired reproducibility,
and all three controller baselines have regression coverage. Run the focused
suite from `mujoco_force_control/` with
`python3 -m pytest -q tests/test_peg_in_hole.py tests/test_peg_episode.py tests/test_stage_b_benchmark.py`.
See the README for benchmark settings, commands, classification rules and
limitations.
On the ten paired held-out seeds 10-19, position achieved 8 successes, hybrid
10, and impedance 9; failures and earlier untuned pilot outcomes are retained.
A five-seed wider-pose stress run produced 4, 5, and 5 successes respectively.
One position trial peaked at 44.9 N despite the 5 N stop because stopping
occurs after a physics step. This value and the benchmark's smaller observed
peaks confirm that the threshold is only a termination condition, not a force
cap. The small, oracle-aligned simulator samples do not validate hardware
safety or establish comparative performance. Stage B is complete for the
simulation-only scope: scene, evaluator, seeded reset, noise/latency model,
classical baselines, traceable metrics, and regression coverage are implemented.
Use more trials and broader evaluation before making comparative-performance
claims; Stage C action conversion and data readiness remain separate gates
before training.

### Stage C: Integrate pi0.5 and collect demonstrations

Simulation integration prototype status: camera/state preprocessing,
non-actuating preview, bounded action postprocessing, local pi05_droid
inference, guarded simulation rollout, synchronized recording, and
training-only feature statistics are implemented and regression-tested.
OpenPI's pinned configuration returns 15x8 actions. The rollout records
p50/p95/maximum inference times and deadline misses against an eight-action
chunk budget. The earlier 58-call trial re-inferred after every action; its
p50 was 80.7 ms and p95 was 83.5 ms against the 66.7 ms single-action period.
Using eight actions per inference matches the pinned OpenPI DROID example.
A warmed local pi0.5 rollout made 8 calls for 60 actions over 4 s of sim time
(p50 84.0 ms, p95 86.4 ms, max 86.7 ms; 0/8 exceeded the 533 ms chunk
budget), but timed out without a successful insertion. The cold first call
exceeded the 5 s client timeout. The simulator still pauses synchronously at
chunk boundaries, so this is not proof of 15 Hz wall-clock control.

Stage C is not complete for training or deployment. The existing pi0.5
contact run dropped the peg. Classical actuator controls are not DROID action
labels, and the tested achieved-motion/replay-fitted conversions still have
peg-tip errors larger than the 0.08 mm clearance scale. The development set
is small and does not establish a representative successful demonstration
dataset. The collector now fits training-only mean/std statistics for joint
position, joint velocity, gripper-closed fraction, and local wrench, with a
1e-6 standard-deviation floor; these auxiliary statistics do not replace the
pretrained checkpoint's own OpenPI transforms.

An oracle-guided scripted teacher has since been implemented with privileged
MuJoCo geometry and the rollout's bounded 0.2-radian joint-delta pipeline.
It completed 10/10 local simulator checks over seeds 0–9 with randomized
fixture offsets and tilts; a three-seed regression test verifies the saved
normalized action reconstructs the joint target actually executed. These are
synthetic simulator labels, not human demonstrations or evidence of transfer.
The new `stage_c_oracle_v2` collection has 2,466 observations over disjoint
20/5/5 train/validation/test trials. It has 2,046 complete 15-step action
windows (1,361 train, 340 validation, 345 test); all 30 trials passed
simulator checks and action-label reconstruction was exact. It is about 5.9x
the earlier training sample count, but remains narrow and simulator-only,
and still needs conversion to OpenPI's DROID/LeRobot dataset layout.

The pinned `pi05_droid` configuration uses Gemma variants; the same OpenPI
revision includes Gemma terms separate from its Apache source license. Review
the current Gemma terms and any checkpoint-provider terms before fine-tuning
or redistribution; this repository cannot confirm a user's acceptance or
provide legal advice. No physical robot is in scope, so hardware validation
is not applicable and must not be inferred from simulation. See the project
README for data, timing, license and remaining training-readiness limitations.

### Stage D: Train the residual policy and ablations

- Implement the residual policy over the fixed pi0.5 nominal action.
- Train all four 2x2 variants with matched data, compute budget, and seeds.
- Version code, model checkpoints, configs, normalization, reward weights, and
  random seeds for every run.
- Select checkpoints using a fixed validation protocol; reserve real-world test
  trials for final evaluation.

### Stage E: Evaluate in simulation and on the real robot

- Evaluate the four variants plus nominal-only and classical baselines in
  simulation, using paired randomized scenarios.
- Progress to real trials only after safety checks and predefined simulator
  criteria pass.
- Run randomized, paired, blinded-to-evaluator real-robot trials when feasible.
- Stop on force/torque limits, abnormal contact, dropped peg, fixture movement,
  sensor faults, or emergency-stop conditions.
- Report trial counts, exclusions, seeds, and all failures.

### Stage F: Analyze and report

- Primary outcome: independently adjudicated insertion success rate.
- Secondary outcomes: lateral error, depth error, peak and integrated force,
  force-limit events, completion time, retries, drops, and damage/failure events.
- Compute peak contact force and intervals continuously above Fref, from
  episode start through task completion for successful trials and through the
  episode end for failed trials. Distinguish Fref from the hard safety limit.
- Classify each failure independently by geometric and force mode, and report
  their counts by variant:
  - Geometric mode (tool's final location): entry failure if aligned over the
    hole but not entered; surface miss if still on the top surface without
    alignment; drift-off if it has left the top surface.
  - Force mode (contact while the held part is on the mating part's top
    surface): excessive pressing if force stays above Fref for at least 1 s
    continuously; unstable contact if surface contact lasts less than 1 s or
    lateral force exceeds axial force; stalled otherwise.
- Specify Fref, contact detection, force component frame and magnitude
  conventions, filtering, and sampling before analysis. Since excessive
  pressing and unstable contact can overlap, predefine whether to report
  multiple labels or use a precedence rule before plotting exclusive bars.
- Estimate observation main effect, reward main effect, and their interaction
  with confidence intervals; account for seed and trial/pose variability.
- Compare against nominal-only and classical baselines; report safety events,
  not only successful trials.
- State limitations and separate simulation findings from real-world claims.

## 8. Reproducibility and Deliverables

- Versioned task specification and success evaluator.
- Simulator configuration and randomized test suite.
- pi0.5 adapter, residual-policy code, and four ablation configurations.
- Demonstration/data schema, collection protocol, and calibrated sensor metadata.
- Training and evaluation scripts, seeds, checkpoints, and normalization stats.
- Trial-level results, analysis notebook/script, plots, and final report.

## 9. Decision Gates

1. Do not train until task geometry and success thresholds are unambiguous.
2. Do not run real contact trials until sensing, frame transforms, action limits,
   and independent safety stops pass bench tests.
3. Do not claim real-world improvement without held-out, randomized real-robot
   trials and uncertainty estimates.
4. Treat the force-on pi0.5 observation path as unverified until the chosen
   model interface and fine-tuning method are demonstrated end to end.